{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-startup-equity-valuation-and-cap-tables-for-investors",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 3: Reading and Building a Cap Table",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "The ESOP Pool: How It Dilutes You Before You Even Invest",
          "slug": "the-esop-pool-how-it-dilutes-you-before-you-even-invest",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "k1",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A Series A term sheet arrives for a startup you backed at seed. The headline looks great: ₹10 crore at a ₹40 crore pre-money valuation. Then, halfway down page two, a single line: \"The Company shall create an unallocated ESOP pool equal to 10% of the post-money fully diluted capital, included in the pre-money valuation.\" That sentence quietly moves ₹5 crore of value away from you and the founders before the new investor pays a rupee. This lesson explains how ESOP pools work in India, why investors want them, and how the timing of a pool decides who pays for it.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "Everyone agrees startups need an ESOP pool. The real negotiation is about who pays for it. A pool created inside the pre-money valuation is paid for entirely by existing shareholders, including you.",
              "_key": "k3"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k4",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k5",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "What an ESOP Pool Is and Why It Exists",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k6",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k7",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Startups cannot match the salaries that large companies pay, so they offer a slice of future upside instead. An ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k9",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "employee stock option",
                  "marks": [
                    "k8"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k10",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " lets an employee buy shares at a fixed price after working for a set period. The ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k12",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "ESOP pool",
                  "marks": [
                    "k11"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k13",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " is the block of shares reserved for these options.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "k8",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A right granted to an employee to buy a set number of company shares at a fixed exercise price, once the option has vested."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k11",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The total number of shares a company has reserved for employee stock options, both granted and not yet granted."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "k14",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k15",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Approval.",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k16",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Under Section 62(1)(b) of the Companies Act, 2013 and Rule 12 of the Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules, 2014, an ESOP scheme needs a special resolution of shareholders.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k17",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k18",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Minimum vesting.",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k19",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " There must be at least one year between grant and vesting.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k20",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k21",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Who can receive options.",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k22",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Independent directors cannot. Promoters and directors holding more than 10% generally cannot either, but DPIIT-recognised startups can grant them options for up to 10 years from incorporation.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_type": "timeline",
              "title": "The life of one employee option (a typical structure)",
              "events": [
                {
                  "_key": "k24",
                  "date": "Joining",
                  "title": "Grant",
                  "description": "A new product lead is granted 8,000 options at an exercise price of ₹10 per share, vesting over four years."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k26",
                  "date": "Year 1",
                  "title": "Cliff",
                  "description": "After a one-year cliff, 25% of the options, 2,000, vest at once. Leave before this and nothing vests."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k28",
                  "date": "Years 2 to 4",
                  "title": "Monthly or quarterly vesting",
                  "description": "The remaining 6,000 options vest in equal instalments until all 8,000 have vested."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k30",
                  "date": "Exercise",
                  "title": "Buying the shares",
                  "description": "The employee pays ₹10 per share to convert vested options into shares, and is taxed on the gap between fair market value and ₹10 as a salary perquisite."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k32",
                  "date": "Liquidity",
                  "title": "Buyback, secondary sale or IPO",
                  "description": "The employee turns shares into cash, paying capital gains tax on any rise above the fair market value at exercise."
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k33"
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "insight",
              "text": "ESOPs are not theoretical in India. Companies such as Flipkart and Swiggy have run ESOP buybacks that let employees convert vested options into cash years before any IPO, which is a big reason senior talent is willing to join startups at lower salaries.",
              "_key": "k34"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k35",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k36",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Why Investors Insist on a Pool",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k37",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k38",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A Series A investor is betting that the company will hire a CTO, a head of sales and fifty engineers over the next two years. Without options to offer, those hires will cost more cash or will not happen. So the investor wants a pool big enough to cover the hiring plan until the next round. A post-money unallocated pool of around 10% is a common ask at Series A, though the right size depends on the hiring plan.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k39",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k40",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Nobody argues about whether a pool is needed. The argument is about when it is created: before the investor comes in, or after.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k41",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k42",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The Option Pool Shuffle: A Worked Example",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k43",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k44",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Kirana Konnect Private Limited is a fictional Nagpur startup whose app lets kirana stores order stock from wholesalers. Before its Series A it has 10,00,000 fully diluted shares.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Kirana Konnect before Series A",
              "headers": [
                "Holder",
                "Shares",
