              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "As a small angel, you are an existing shareholder in every later round, and you rarely get a seat at the negotiating table. The option pool shuffle is one of the most common ways early investors lose value without anyone mentioning it. When a round is announced, ask the founders directly: \"How big is the new pool, and is it in the pre-money?\"",
              "_key": "k154"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k155",
              "_type": "collapsible",
              "title": "How ESOPs are taxed for employees in India",
              "content": [
                {
                  "_key": "k156",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "k157",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Employees are typically taxed at two points:",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "markDefs": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k158",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "k159",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "At exercise.",
                      "marks": [
                        "strong"
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "_key": "k160",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": " The gap between the fair market value on the exercise date and the exercise price is taxed as a salary perquisite. For unlisted companies, fair market value is determined by a SEBI-registered merchant banker.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k161",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "k162",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "At sale.",
                      "marks": [
                        "strong"
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "_key": "k163",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": " Any gain above the fair market value on the exercise date is taxed as capital gains.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k164",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "k165",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Employees of eligible DPIIT-recognised startups may be able to defer the tax payable at exercise until a later trigger, such as a sale, leaving the company or a set period expiring. The Income-tax Act, 2025 applies from 1 April 2026, so confirm current provisions and section references with a chartered accountant.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "markDefs": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "fillInTheBlank",
              "title": "Name the tactic",
              "textWithBlank": "Creating a new ESOP pool inside the pre-money valuation, so that only existing shareholders are diluted, is known as the option pool ___.",
              "correctAnswers": [
                "shuffle"
              ],
              "explanation": "The shuffle lets a new investor quote a higher headline pre-money valuation while existing holders receive a lower effective one.",
              "_key": "k166"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "scenario",
              "title": "The 15% pool demand",
              "scenario": "You invested ₹15 lakh in a Bengaluru B2B SaaS startup at seed. It is now negotiating a ₹20 crore Series A at an ₹80 crore pre-money valuation. The lead investor wants a 15% post-money unallocated pool inside the pre-money. The company already has 5% unallocated. The founders share their hiring plan: a CTO, a VP Sales and around 20 engineers over 24 months, which they estimate needs about 8% of post-money capital in new grants.",
              "prompt": "What is wrong with the 15% demand, and what would you encourage the founders to negotiate?",
              "modelAnswer": "What is wrong:\n1. The pool is sized well above the hiring plan. The plan needs about 8%, and 5% already sits unallocated. A top-up of about 3% would cover the plan, not a 15% target.\n2. Because the pool sits inside the pre-money, you and the founders pay for all of it. At a ₹100 crore post-money, 15% is ₹15 crore of value. If it is all new options, the effective pre-money falls from ₹80 crore to ₹65 crore. Even counting the existing 5%, the new 10% top-up is worth ₹10 crore, taking the effective pre-money to ₹70 crore.\n3. Unused options stay in the pool until the next round, so the extra dilution may never buy any hires.\n\nWhat to negotiate:\n1. Size the post-money unallocated pool to the hiring plan, around 8%, and count the existing 5% towards it.\n2. Ask for any top-up beyond the hiring plan to be created in the post-money, so the new investor shares the dilution.\n3. If the investor refuses, trade: accept the larger pool only with a higher headline pre-money that keeps the effective valuation near ₹80 crore.\n4. Agree that the pool is refreshed at Series B, when each option costs less ownership.",
              "_key": "k167"
            },
            {
              "_type": "flashcardSet",
              "title": "ESOP Pools",
              "cards": [
                {
                  "_key": "k169",
                  "term": "ESOP pool",
                  "definition": "Shares reserved for employee stock options, including options not yet granted."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k171",
                  "term": "Vesting",
                  "definition": "Earning the right to exercise options over time. Indian rules require at least one year between grant and vesting."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k173",
                  "term": "Cliff",
                  "definition": "An initial period, commonly one year, before which no options vest."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k175",
                  "term": "Exercise price",
                  "definition": "The fixed price an employee pays to convert a vested option into a share."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k177",
                  "term": "Option pool shuffle",
                  "definition": "Creating a pool inside the pre-money valuation so only existing shareholders bear its dilution."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k179",
                  "term": "Effective pre-money",
                  "definition": "Headline pre-money minus the value of new pool shares at the round price."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k181",
                  "term": "ESOP buyback",
                  "definition": "A company or investor buying vested employee shares or options for cash, giving employees liquidity before an IPO."
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k182"
            },
            {
              "_type": "keyTakeaways",
              "points": [
                "ESOP pools let startups attract talent with equity instead of cash, under Section 62(1)(b) and Rule 12, with a minimum one-year vesting period.",
                "Investors usually require an unallocated pool large enough to cover hiring until the next round.",
                "A pool created inside the pre-money valuation is paid for entirely by existing shareholders. This is the option pool shuffle.",
                "Effective pre-money = headline pre-money minus the value of the new pool at the round price.",
                "Push for a pool sized to a real hiring plan, count existing unallocated options, and refresh at later rounds.",
                "As an early investor, always ask how big a new pool is and whether it sits in the pre-money or post-money."
